\bar defines a bar, with a melodic line for each instrument.
The instruments must be created with \insts.init first.
Its syntax is the following:

\bar name {
    \time 4/4
    \inst1 c'4 d' e' f'
    \inst2 c2 g
}

The name is given without the backslash, and once the bar
is defined it works like a command, by prepending the
backslash. Calling it alone in a line makes it the bar
to be played:

\name

A bar can also be written in one line, and it can be
called right after its closing bracket:

\bar name { \time 4/4 \inst1 c'4 d' e' f' \inst2 c1 } \name

The time signature and the tempo of the previous bar are
kept if the bar doesn't set its own. An instrument that is
left out of a bar gets a whole bar rest.

An instrument can copy its line from another bar, or the
line of another instrument of that bar:

\bar name2 {
    \inst1 \name
    \inst2 \name.\inst1
}

A bar name can't be redefined, it can't start with a
backslash, and it can't be the name of a LiveLily command
(e.g. \bar merge is an error, as \merge is a command).
A bar can't copy itself.
